327	  <section class="chapter right" id="ch1" aria-label="The confound">
328	    <div class="ch-kick">Chapter 01 — the confound</div>
329	    <h2 class="display ch-h"><span class="hollow">Shape changed.</span><br>So did supper.</h2>
330	    <p class="ch-copy">An organoid differs from a classic flat culture in <b>two</b> ways at once: its
331	    3D shape — and its diet. Flat cultures bathe in serum, rich in lipids and cholesterol. Organoids
332	    get a precise, serum-free recipe. So when a gene suddenly looks essential in organoids, is that
333	    the shape talking… <b>or starvation?</b></p>
334	    <p class="ch-copy" style="margin-top:12px">Luckily, the public data contain an observational control: other
335	    models grown <b>flat, but fed organoid-type medium</b>. Food changed, shape didn&rsquo;t. That single
336	    comparison — observational, with caveats detailed below — splits the story in two.</p>
337	    <div class="stage-caption" style="right:auto;left:5vw;text-align:left">Left: grown flat.<br>Right: grown as a 3D dome.<br><b>The question is which difference matters.</b></div>
338	  </section>
